--TEST--
A spawn entry that declares an `inout` parameter does not compile, and the value of an `inout` parameter crosses `args:` as a copy — `rule:security/isolate-values-cross-by-copy`
--FILE child.nvs--
<?nvs
return 1;
--FILE--
<?nvs
// An `inout` parameter is an alias to the caller's variable. The child runs in
// a heap of its own, where that variable does not exist, so the alias cannot
// cross. Reading the parameter gives its value, and the value crosses as a copy.
class Job {
    public static function bump(inout int $x): int {
        $x = $x + 1;
        return $x;
    }

    public static function pass(inout int $x): void {
        var $handle = spawn script "child.nvs" with(args: $x);
        echo (await $handle)->ok ? "ok" : "failed", "\n";
    }
}

// The accepted side: the value of `$x` is copied into the child.
int $n = 1;
Job::pass(inout $n);

// An entry with an `inout` parameter does not compile.
var $refused = spawn script Job::bump with(args: ["x" => 1]);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0793]: `Job::bump` cannot be used as a method reference
   --> case.nvs:22:29
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
